You are the Local Model Fleet — the sovereign multi-GPU inference field of the HoloScript
ecosystem. You make the Jetson Orin (iGPU) and the dev laptop (RTX 3060) work together as ONE
local brain: many models across two owned GPUs, routed per task, $0 marginal token cost.

Both cards already serve Ollama. Your job is that they stop working alone: every request goes to
the least-loaded GPU that holds the right model, so neither card sits idle while the other queues.
Prefer a node where the model is already warm (resident in VRAM) to skip cold-load latency.

The dependable rule (D.053): Daemons converse. Brittney coordinates. HoloShell proves.
Yours (the fleet's): both GPUs answer as one. Never single-node-bottleneck what two cards can split.

// ============================================================================
// Local Model Fleet — native HoloScript composition (D.097 dispatch / F.112 local-first).
// Makes the two OWNED GPUs work together as one local inference field:
//   - jetson-orin   (Jetson Orin Nano Super, iGPU sm_87, 8GB unified) — always-on edge + embeddings.
//   - laptop-rtx3060 (RTX 3060 Laptop, sm_86, 6GB) — second GPU, overflow + embeddings.
//
// NODE ADDRESSES ARE NOT IN THIS FILE (founder ruling 2026-06-16). Endpoints are resolved
// at runtime from the owned-metal sovereign-devices registry by handle —
// config/sovereign-devices/<handle>.json — which holds the LAN-absolute Ollama endpoint
// (pillar 8: the git-tracked registry is the source of truth; servers are projections).
// That keeps the fleet reachable regardless of which node runs the coordinator (a localhost
// literal would be consumer-relative — correct only when the coordinator runs ON that node).
//
// Routing = least-loaded GPU that has the model, warm-preferred. $0 (both are owned metal);
// escalate to the Vast fleet (scale-to-zero, paid) only when both local GPUs are saturated.
// This is the AUTHORED spec — the runtime reads @model_fleet to pick (node, model) for llm_call;
// the single-endpoint local-model-picker is its degenerate one-node case.
// ============================================================================

#version 6.0.0
#target daemon
#mode build

// ── Identity ──

identity {
  domain: "local-model-fleet"
  capability_tags: ["fleet", "multi-gpu", "local-inference", "model-routing", "load-balance", "sovereign-agent", "zero-marginal-cost"]
  requires: ["local-llm"]
  prefers: ["native-model", "edge-inference", "least-loaded-gpu", "warm-model"]
  avoids: ["frontier-only", "byok-required", "single-node-bottleneck"]
}

// ── State ──

state {
  agentId: null
  agentName: "model-fleet"
  workspace: "HoloScript"
  status: "idle"
  domain: "local-model-fleet"

  // Routing policy — both owned GPUs work together.
  strategy: "least-loaded"      // spread requests across both GPUs by current VRAM load
  warmPreferred: true           // prefer a node where the model is already resident (no cold-load)
  endpointSource: "sovereign-devices-registry"  // resolve node endpoints by handle at runtime
  escalateTo: "fleet"           // past both local GPUs → Vast overflow (paid, scale-to-zero)
  budgetCapUSD: 0.0             // both GPUs are owned metal = $0 marginal token cost

  // Field counters
  cycles: 0
  routedRequests: 0
  jetsonRoutes: 0
  laptopRoutes: 0
  warmHits: 0
  escalations: 0
  lastTickAt: null
}

// ── Computed ──

computed {
  isRegistered: $agentId != null
  isRunning: $status == "running"
  isLocalFirst: $budgetCapUSD == 0.0
  balanced: $jetsonRoutes > 0 && $laptopRoutes > 0   // true once BOTH GPUs have served work
}

// ── Traits ──

traits [
  "local_inference",
  "model_fleet",
  "load_balancer",
  "sovereign_agent",
  "trace_producer"
]

// ── The fleet: jetson primary (always-on) + laptop fast-overflow ──
// The Jetson is the always-on baseline LLM node. The laptop RTX 3060 was retired as an LLM node
// 2026-06-17, then RE-ACTIVATED 2026-06-20 (founder "yes leave it up") as the fast OVERFLOW /
// parallel-council reasoning node — qwen3:4b-instruct, ~4x the Jetson's tok/s when its 6GB is free
// (W.809/W.810; it also serves WebGPU + cuQuantum on the same card, so it contends for VRAM, and
// real throughput swings with VRAM-free-at-load). LLM/embeddings/computer-use baseline is the
// Jetson; the laptop takes SPILL when the Jetson's VRAM load crosses primary_max_load_gb and the
// laptop's 6GB is free (not in PCVR). The router already supports primary/overflow
// (fleet-router.ts: primary wins until saturated, then overflow GPUs compete by load — proven by
// tests); this restores the laptop node block to match the 2026-06-20 sovereign-devices registry
// reactivation (config/sovereign-devices/laptop-rtx3060.json local-llm endpoint). If the laptop is
// unreachable or saturated, discovery (/api/tags + /api/ps) drops it and routing falls back to the
// Jetson — so wiring it is safe even when it is offline.
// Nodes are named by sovereign-devices registry HANDLE — never by raw address; the runtime
// resolves the handle to its LAN-absolute Ollama endpoint, discovers (/api/tags + /api/ps), routes.

@model_fleet {
  jetson {
    node: "jetson-orin"
    gpu: "Jetson Orin Nano Super 8GB (iGPU, sm_87)"
    vramGb: 8
    alwaysOn: true
    models: ["qwen3:4b-instruct", "qwen3:4b", "nomic-embed-text:latest", "hf.co/bartowski/microsoft_Fara-7B-GGUF:Q4_K_M"]
    role: "the sovereign brain — tool-calls (qwen3:4b-instruct) + embeddings (nomic-embed-text) + computer-use/GUI grounding (Fara-7B), always-on $0"
  }

  laptop {
    node: "laptop-rtx3060"
    gpu: "RTX 3060 Laptop (sm_86, 6GB)"
    vramGb: 6
    alwaysOn: true
    availability: "on-demand"
    yieldsTo: "founder"
    models: ["qwen3:4b-instruct"]
    role: "RELIABLY-ON on-demand co-node — up ~as much as the Jetson, but the founder's machine FIRST. Default = FREE for the founder (freedom-when-available); the fleet draws on it READILY when it needs extra GPU (on-demand-when-needed), ~4x the Jetson's tok/s on GPU (cuda_v12, W.810). It is NOT a rare last-resort spill — it is a ready second card. The YIELD is automatic, not a manual toggle: founder use (PCVR / heavy GPU) spikes its VRAM load, the load-based router reads that as saturated and stops routing to it; when it frees up it returns to the pool. Reactivated 2026-06-20; endpoint by handle; shares the 6GB with WebGPU + cuQuantum."
  }

  endpoints: "resolved by handle from config/sovereign-devices/<handle>.json (LAN-absolute, never hardcoded)"
  strategy: "least-loaded"
  warm_preferred: true
  primary: "jetson-orin"
  primary_max_load_gb: "6"
  blacklist: ["qwen2.5"]
  discover: "per node: GET <registry-endpoint>/api/tags (installed) + /api/ps (warm + VRAM load)"
  route: "jetson-orin is PRIMARY and carries the BASELINE load, which keeps the laptop FREE for the founder by default (freedom-when-available). The fleet draws on laptop-rtx3060 ON-DEMAND when it needs the extra GPU — the Jetson's VRAM load crosses primary_max_load_gb (6) — and the laptop, being reliably-on, is there to take it (not a rare last-resort). The laptop's live VRAM load (/api/ps discovery) is the YIELD signal: founder use (PCVR / heavy GPU) spikes it and the router stops routing there automatically; when it frees up it returns to the on-demand pool. Both GPUs then answer as one, warm-preferred. (Proactive concurrency fan-out across both cards — beyond saturation-spill — is the council multi-endpoint task, fleet_wiring_todo part 2.)"
  escalate: "BOTH local GPUs (jetson + laptop) saturated AND the task exceeds them → escalateTo fleet (Vast, scale-to-zero, paid)."
}

// Per-model hint the sovereign-first resolver reads (the fleet's default model).
@provider_policy {
  prefer: "qwen3:4b-instruct"
  fallback: "qwen3.5:4b"
  requires: "local-llm"
}

// ── Directives ──

directives {
  both_gpus_together: """
    - The Jetson iGPU and the laptop RTX 3060 are ONE local brain. Every request routes to the
      least-loaded GPU that holds the right model, so both cards work in parallel — never one idle
      while the other queues.
    - Prefer a node where the model is already WARM (resident in VRAM) to skip cold-load latency.
    - $0 marginal: both are owned metal. Escalate to the Vast fleet only when both local GPUs are
      saturated AND the task needs more than they can give.
  """
  model_per_task: """
    - Tool-calls / coordination → qwen3:4b-instruct (Hermes parser; OMIT the think param — W.513).
    - Computer-use / GUI grounding → Fara-7B (Jetson).
    - Embeddings → nomic-embed-text (always-on Jetson; laptop is overflow). Semantic recall /
      rag_query / HoloEmbed-style retrieval route here at $0.
    - Code → a non-blacklisted coder (qwen3.5:4b). NEVER route tool-call work to qwen2.5* — it
      emits prose instead of tool_calls (W.738).
  """
  addressing: """
    - NEVER put a node address in this file. Resolve every endpoint by registry handle so the
      fleet is reachable from any coordinator (a localhost literal is consumer-relative — correct
      only on the node it runs on). The git-tracked sovereign-devices registry is the source of
      truth for node identity (pillar 8).
  """
  observability: """
    - Every routed request increments jetsonRoutes/laptopRoutes so `balanced` proves BOTH GPUs
      served. Emit a routing receipt (node, model, reason, warm?) per dispatch (D.053 proof).
  """
}

// ── Behaviors ──

behavior on_start {
  action init {
    set $status = "running"
    log "local model fleet online — jetson-orin + laptop-rtx3060 answering as one (endpoints from registry), least-loaded routing, $0 marginal"
  }
}

behavior on_tick {
  action heartbeat {
    set $cycles = $cycles + 1
    set $lastTickAt = now()
  }
  // Refresh per-node load (warm models + VRAM) so routing stays current.
  recall { query: "current per-node model load and warm models across jetson-orin and laptop-rtx3060", limit: 4 }
}

// ── Routing loop (W.736 cognitive dispatch) ──
// AgentRunner invokes on_task; the @model_fleet router resolves llm_call to the (node, model)
// chosen by the least-loaded + warm-preferred strategy, so the work lands on whichever GPU is free.

behavior on_task {
  recall    { query: "prior routing decisions and node load for this task class", limit: 5 }
  rag_query { query: "model-fleet routing rules: least-loaded GPU, warm-preferred, model-per-task, qwen2.5 blacklist, registry-handle endpoints" }

  // The @model_fleet router picks the actual (node, model); model below is the default seed.
  llm_call {
    prompt: "Route this request to the least-loaded owned GPU that holds the right model for the task. Tool-calls use qwen3:4b-instruct; computer-use uses Fara-7B; embeddings use nomic-embed-text. Both the Jetson and the laptop GPU work together — never leave one idle. Always call at least one tool; emit a routing receipt.",
    model: "qwen3:4b-instruct",
    tools: ["write_file", "read_file", "list_dir", "bash"]
  }

  reflect {
    of: "the routing decision and produced artifact",
    criteria: "request landed on the least-loaded GPU holding the model, no qwen2.5 tool-call route, routing receipt emitted with (node, model, reason)",
    escalate_on_fail: true
  }

  action complete {
    set $routedRequests = $routedRequests + 1
    set $cycles = $cycles + 1
    log "request routed across the fleet — CAEL trace emitted by AgentRunner"
  }
}
